- op: discuss  # d7d6
  at: '2026-07-29T16:59:50Z'  # d7d6
  by: agent  # d7d6
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d7d6
  clock: 5  # d7d6
  nonce: d7d6  # d7d6
  message: "OWNER DECISION (2026-07-29, four-step walkthrough). WI-talos is ACCEPTED and now\nhas a complete spec. Recording it here because the sibling WI-banod T1 was parked\nwaiting on exactly this.\n\nPREMISE RE-VERIFIED before deciding (the item is from 2026-06-07 and had drifted):\nthe four declarations now read stableid-v8 / shapeid-v3 / repofp-v2 / symbol-fp-v2,\nand the complaint still holds -- but with TWO corrections to the filed text.\n  (a) symbol_fingerprint ALREADY solves this: it emits `hgfp2:` under `-v2`\n      (it was `hgfp1:` under `-v1` when filed). The prefix tracks the version and\n      fingerprint.py:84 documents that a future change becomes `hgfp3:`. So the\n      pattern being adopted is an EXISTING in-tree precedent, not a new invention.\n  (b) The sharper harm is not the missing version, it is COLLISION: stable_id,\n      shape_id AND repo_fingerprint all emit `sha256:`, so three of the four are\n      mutually indistinguishable TODAY. WI-bosog's repofp change made it three.\n\nDECISION 1 -- values become self-describing, following the hgfp2 precedent.\nEach identity type gets its own versioned prefix. Digests are NOT recomputed;\nonly the label changes.\n\nDECISION 2 -- the version number tracks the emitted FORMAT, so a pure relabel\nBUMPS it (v8 -> v9). To stop consumers from needlessly re-keying stored data on a\nversion change that did not alter a single digest, the output ALSO declares\ndigest-compatibility with the predecessor scheme. Rationale: bumping without that\nmarker is never wrong but wastes consumer effort; not bumping would leave two\nbyte-formats both claiming v8, which is the ambiguity this item exists to remove.\n\nDECISION 3 -- see WI-banod T1. TWO bumps, so every claim stays unconditional:\n  v9  = relabel ONLY. Unqualified digest_compatible_with = v8.\n  v10 = the bitbake/bibtex stable_id fix. NO compatibility declared.\nRejected: folding both into v9 with a per-producer exception list (keeps the\nsignal for ~all consumers but makes the compatibility field carry structure), and\nfolding both in with no stable_id compatibility at all (simple and true, but a\n5-site exception would poison the don't-re-key signal for everyone).\n\nDECISION 4 -- prefixes are READABLE and hg-NAMESPACED:\n    stable_id         hgstableid9:9833bc15f983fc00\n    shape_id          hgshapeid4:9ac2d6ecce3b9943\n    repo_fingerprint  hgrepofp3:<64hex>\n    fingerprint       hgfp2:423086432978e37b        (UNCHANGED -- already correct)\nRejected the mechanical hgfp2-style contraction (`hgsid9:` / `hgshid4:`) because\nthose differ by ONE letter -- and stable_id vs shape_id is precisely the pair this\nchange exists to disambiguate. Cost accepted: ~5 extra chars per identity value\n(~0.5MB on a ~100k-symbol map).\n\nIMPLEMENTATION NOTES (agent-owned, no further owner input needed):\n  * 4 constants in schema.py:123/132/141/148.\n  * spec_validator.py:1826 `_CANONICAL_STABLE_ID_PATTERN = ^sha256:[0-9a-f]{16}$`\n    and the malformed-shape reporter at :1839/:1842.\n  * 63 test files assert on the `sha256:` prefix.\n  * The digest-compatibility declaration is a NEW schema field; I will name and\n    shape it, and bump SCHEMA_VERSION for the additive field.\n  * fingerprint.py needs no change.\n\nASSUMPTION FLAGGED: the owner chose the option worded \"two releases\", which I am\nreading as two SEPARATE scheme bumps that must not ship in one release cycle --\nv10 waits for a release boundary rather than following v9 in the next PR. Say so\nif the intent was merely \"two bumps, sequencing flexible.\""  # d7d6
